WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.000
The natural world is full of extraordinary animals with amazing life histories.

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:13.000
Yet certain stories are more intriguing than most.

00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:22.000
The mysteries of a butterfly's life cycle, or the strange biology of the emperor penguin.

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:29.000
Some of these creatures were surrounded by myth and misunderstandings for a very long time.

00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:34.000
And some have only recently revealed their secrets.

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:38.000
These are the animals that stand out from the crowd.

00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:43.000
The curiosities I find particularly fascinating.

00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:54.000
Some animals can perform amazing physical feats.

00:00:54.000 --> 00:01:00.000
A flea's jump is said to be the equivalent of a man leaping over St Paul's Cathedral.

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:07.000
And it's famously quoted that cheetahs can run at speeds of 70 miles an hour.

00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:28.000
But are these claims really true?

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:38.000
Look, this is a real live flea circus and you can see this one pulling along this tiny chariot.

00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:43.000
There are very few circuses like this these days.

00:01:43.000 --> 00:01:48.000
The whole business of performing fleas dates back into the 16th century.

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:56.000
And it was used by watchmakers who used them to demonstrate how they themselves could work on a near miniature scale.

00:01:56.000 --> 00:02:03.000
They used thin gold wires to harness fleas and then linked the fleas to tiny chains.

00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:10.000
Early magnifying devices like this were actually named flea glasses after these pests.

00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:17.000
And the fleas were excellent creatures to demonstrate a newly visible microscopic world.

00:02:17.000 --> 00:02:21.000
Fleas appear to be extraordinarily strong.

00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:28.000
After all, this little badge of flea here pulling this chariot, what an extraordinary thing.

00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:33.000
That's the equivalent to me trying to pull a jumbo jet single-handed.

00:02:33.000 --> 00:02:41.000
And this tiny merry-go-round, that too is completely powered by fleas.

00:02:41.000 --> 00:02:49.000
The secret of the fleas' strength and ability to move such equipment lies in their powerful walking and jumping techniques.

00:02:49.000 --> 00:02:58.000
They have the ability to store and then release energy, and that enables them to leap upwards with great acceleration.

00:02:58.000 --> 00:03:03.000
Fleas need to be good jumpers.

00:03:03.000 --> 00:03:07.000
They live on the skin of mammals and birds sucking their blood.

00:03:07.000 --> 00:03:13.000
So they have to be able to quickly leap on board their traveling hosts when they get the chance.

00:03:13.000 --> 00:03:21.000
There are more than 2,500 species worldwide, 62 of which live in Britain.

00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:31.000
Fortunately, only a few feed on us.

00:03:31.000 --> 00:03:40.000
Rat fleas were said to be responsible for the spread of the Black Death in 1665, which killed millions.

00:03:40.000 --> 00:03:49.000
But it wasn't until the invention of the magnifying glass that we were able to see these tiny creatures face to face.

00:03:49.000 --> 00:03:58.000
In 1665, Robert Hooke, an inventor and natural philosopher, made one of the first compound microscopes.

00:03:58.000 --> 00:04:01.000
This is a later reproduction of it.

00:04:01.000 --> 00:04:08.000
And he then published his discoveries that he made using it in a marvelous book called Micrographia.

00:04:08.000 --> 00:04:12.000
It became one of the first scientific breast stovers.

00:04:12.000 --> 00:04:15.000
Samuel Pepys mentioned it in his diary.

00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:22.000
And it contained magnificent detailed drawings that reveal biological structures that have never been seen before.

00:04:22.000 --> 00:04:30.000
He saw that plant tissue was made up of little units that he called cells, the word we still use.

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:39.000
And he drew this marvelously detailed flea, showing its great strikingly long legs.

00:04:39.000 --> 00:04:45.000
He also watched it at the microscope and he described how a flea jumped.

00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:48.000
This is what he says.

00:04:48.000 --> 00:04:57.000
When the flea intends to leap, he folds up these six legs together, then springs them all out at the same instant,

00:04:57.000 --> 00:05:05.000
and thereby exerting his whole strength at once, carries his little body to a considerable distance.

00:05:05.000 --> 00:05:07.000
Indeed he does.

00:05:12.000 --> 00:05:21.000
A flea's jump takes just one thousandth of a second, so Hooke must have had very sharp eyesight to see it.

00:05:21.000 --> 00:05:29.000
Many researchers have been fascinated by fleas, and for one particular family they became an obsession.

00:05:29.000 --> 00:05:40.000
Charles Rothschild, a banker and keen naturalist, amassed over 30,000 specimens and identified more than 500 new species.

00:05:40.000 --> 00:05:47.000
He purchased them from specialist traders worldwide,

00:05:47.000 --> 00:05:52.000
and one parcel from America had a special surprise.

00:05:52.000 --> 00:05:56.000
The tiny fleas were dressed as Mexicans.

00:06:04.000 --> 00:06:10.000
Miriam, Charles's daughter, shared his passion for fleas and cataloged his whole collection.

00:06:10.000 --> 00:06:19.000
She looked closely at the flea's body and the way they jumped, and was puzzled to find that they could leap far higher than should theoretically have been possible.

00:06:19.000 --> 00:06:26.000
But could their reputation for jumping 200 times their body length possibly be true?

00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:37.000
Most of the natural world's top jumpers achieve their impressive leaps by using straightforward muscle power.

00:06:37.000 --> 00:06:43.000
Kangaroos can make single bounds of almost 8 meters,

00:06:43.000 --> 00:06:49.000
and frogs are able to jump more than 20 times their body length.

00:06:55.000 --> 00:07:01.000
The jumping spider's leap is even more impressive, a hundred times its own length.

00:07:01.000 --> 00:07:15.000
It achieves this by exploiting hydraulics, and scientists had long suspected that fleas and other insects also needed something other than muscle to make their huge jumps.

00:07:15.000 --> 00:07:30.000
In the 1960s, an exciting discovery was made in the insect world that helped explain how bigger flying insects like locusts and dragonflies were able to fly and jump so well.

00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:37.000
A rubbery protein was found in the hinges and joints of locust wings and legs.

00:07:37.000 --> 00:07:45.000
Using ultraviolet light, it's possible to see it, as in this picture of the joint of a locust.

00:07:45.000 --> 00:07:49.000
Here, that blue is this new substance.

00:07:49.000 --> 00:07:55.000
But just like this rubber, it could bend and then release energy.

00:07:55.000 --> 00:08:00.000
But the newly discovered material did that with more than 90% efficiency.

00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:07.000
Remarkably, too, it repeatedly snapped back into shape without any deformation.

00:08:07.000 --> 00:08:10.000
It was named resilin.

00:08:10.000 --> 00:08:19.000
This stretchy protein allows insects to bend their stiff bodies and stretch their tendons without snapping.

00:08:19.000 --> 00:08:30.000
It's so robust, it lasts a lifetime, and it's believed to be the most efficient elastic protein known.

00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:45.000
The discovery of resilin opened up a whole new area of study, and in 1966, Henry Bennett Clark, an expert in insect biomechanics, had a breakthrough moment.

00:08:45.000 --> 00:08:54.000
He had the chance to see some exciting new footage of fleas shot on a newly invented high-speed camera.

00:08:54.000 --> 00:09:02.000
Bennett Clark studied the new flea footage and built a mechanical model 400 times bigger than the flea.

00:09:02.000 --> 00:09:09.000
He calculated that the fleas were somehow generating much more power than their muscles could actually provide.

00:09:09.000 --> 00:09:20.000
He noticed that just before leaping, the flea bent the closest segment of its hindmost legs towards the body and hesitated for about a tenth of a second.

00:09:20.000 --> 00:09:28.000
Carefully, he dissected fleas and found a pad of material, and that proved to be resilin.

00:09:28.000 --> 00:09:40.000
He proposed that fleas stored some of the energy for their jumps in this rubber-like tissue and then released it as they pushed off with their shins and feet.

00:09:40.000 --> 00:09:50.000
So the tiny wingless fleas use internal resilin springs like those of other bigger flying and jumping insects.

00:09:50.000 --> 00:10:03.000
And the secret of their huge leaps lies in the efficient way they combine muscle, tendons and joints to harness the resilin's energy.

00:10:03.000 --> 00:10:08.000
Only today do we know how a flea jumps and how high it can jump.

00:10:08.000 --> 00:10:16.000
Just as in Hook's time, a modern technology, a microscope, enabled him to see the anatomy of the flea for the very first time,

00:10:16.000 --> 00:10:25.000
so we have a camera now which is recording 5,000 images a second which will enable us to see how it jumps.

00:10:25.000 --> 00:10:31.000
The camera is already running. The flea is in that little box there.

00:10:31.000 --> 00:10:42.000
And we can see the image from the camera on this computer. I will stop it as soon as I see the flea has jumped.

00:10:42.000 --> 00:10:46.000
There.

00:10:46.000 --> 00:10:56.000
Its legs are already cocked in the jumping position and the cuticle which is fused to the resilin is bent and ready to release its energy.

00:10:56.000 --> 00:11:05.000
And then it lifts itself from the ground and it's catapotted into the air.

00:11:05.000 --> 00:11:12.000
Our story about fleas started 350 years ago with Robert Hook's first microscopic study.

00:11:12.000 --> 00:11:20.000
Today, images from electron microscopes reveal even more details than Hook's beautiful drawings.

00:11:20.000 --> 00:11:30.000
They show the rough hairs on the flea's shins and toes that help it grip before thrusting itself into the air with a final push from its toes.

00:11:30.000 --> 00:11:37.000
So, can fleas jump 200 times their own body length? It would seem not.

00:11:37.000 --> 00:11:46.000
Nonetheless, they can leap a respectable 38 times the length of their bodies, which is not bad.

00:11:46.000 --> 00:11:53.000
Fleas are extraordinarily strong and we now know how they jump.

00:11:53.000 --> 00:12:02.000
But the heavy story isn't quite over. A new discovery has added a twist to their lives and dispelled another myth.

00:12:02.000 --> 00:12:10.000
Recently, bodies of people who died of the Black Death were uncovered by workers digging a new railway line.

00:12:10.000 --> 00:12:18.000
Clare's inspection revealed that the Black Death was an airborne disease and had nothing to do with rats or their fleas.

00:12:18.000 --> 00:12:28.000
So the fleas' good name can at last be restored and we can celebrate them as one of the natural world's most spectacular chompers.

00:12:28.000 --> 00:12:43.000
Next, meet an animal that performs another amazing feat. The cheetah, that they say, can run at over 70 miles an hour.

00:12:43.000 --> 00:12:49.000
Fleas can't jump as high as some say, but they can make extraordinary leaps.

00:12:49.000 --> 00:12:58.000
Next, we investigate another amazing claim, the cheetah's ability to run at speeds of 70 miles an hour.

00:12:58.000 --> 00:13:03.000
Is that really possible?

00:13:03.000 --> 00:13:17.000
Cheetahs are beautiful, athletic-looking cats. They've got streamlined body, small head, elongated legs, and narrow shoulders and a very long spine.

00:13:17.000 --> 00:13:25.000
This looks like an animal that's built for speed. But exactly how fast can they run?

00:13:25.000 --> 00:13:33.000
They've been admired for their grace and speed since antiquity.

00:13:33.000 --> 00:13:41.000
The Egyptians were sometimes buried with these cats because they believed that they could hasten the journey into the afterworld.

00:13:41.000 --> 00:13:50.000
And in more recent times, sports hunters have used cheetahs as a way to get to the surface of the desert.

00:13:50.000 --> 00:13:58.000
And in more recent times, sports hunters have used cheetahs to run down their prey.

00:14:00.000 --> 00:14:11.000
So the cheetah certainly has an impressive sprint. But where did the magical figure of 70 miles an hour come from?

00:14:11.000 --> 00:14:21.000
Back in 1957, a cheetah hit the headlines with news of a rather unusual experiment.

00:14:28.000 --> 00:14:38.000
A photographer called Kurt Severin filmed and measured the running speed of a tame cheetah using an upturned bicycle rather like this.

00:14:38.000 --> 00:14:47.000
The back wheel was modified so that a strong fishing line could be wound through the rim and pull along a meat-scented bag.

00:14:47.000 --> 00:14:59.000
As the cheetah ran the 80-yard of 73-meter course, the pedals of the bike were hand-cranked as fast as humanly possible to drag the bag along just ahead of the cheetah.

00:14:59.000 --> 00:15:05.000
The measurements were made manually using a stopwatch and a pistol.

00:15:05.000 --> 00:15:16.000
Severin wrote that from a deep crouch, the cheetah spurted to the end of the course in 2.25 seconds for an average speed of 71 miles an hour.

00:15:16.000 --> 00:15:19.000
So the legend was born.

00:15:21.000 --> 00:15:29.000
This impressive figure was immediately accepted and is still often quoted today. But how accurate is it?

00:15:29.000 --> 00:15:37.000
The top speed of any running mammal depends on the power of its muscles and the strength of its tendons and bones.

00:15:38.000 --> 00:15:46.000
Human athletes train hard to reach their personal best, but there's still a limit to how fast they can run.

00:15:47.000 --> 00:15:55.000
In a 100-meter sprint, a mere 2 seconds separates a good amateur sprinter from a world-class champion.

00:15:55.000 --> 00:16:07.000
The greyhound is similar in size and shape to a cheetah, so it's a good substitute animal to test out the cheetah's legendary top speed of 70 miles an hour.

00:16:10.000 --> 00:16:17.000
Their backs flex and extend so greatly that at times none of their feet touch the ground.

00:16:17.000 --> 00:16:26.000
But when the greyhound's top speed was measured, it was found to be 45 miles an hour, a whole 25 miles an hour slower than the cheetah.

00:16:30.000 --> 00:16:38.000
People argued that the cheetah could nonetheless achieve a bigger stride because of extra flexibility in its back.

00:16:38.000 --> 00:16:45.000
But doubts about its top speed were beginning to creep in. A new, more accurate way of testing was needed.

00:16:48.000 --> 00:16:54.000
Here in the Royal Veterinary College, they use dogs to help them in their studies of cheetahs.

00:16:54.000 --> 00:17:01.000
Using a lurcher as a stand-in, they've developed an extraordinary data-collecting collar.

00:17:01.000 --> 00:17:07.000
It has a GPS attachment that will register position to within a fraction of a metre.

00:17:07.000 --> 00:17:11.000
It has movement sensors to show how the animal is in fact moving.

00:17:11.000 --> 00:17:18.000
It can be remotely programmed and it has a solar-charged battery that will last for up to a year.

00:17:19.000 --> 00:17:25.000
The collars were tested and perfected on lurchers in Britain and were tested in the UK.

00:17:25.000 --> 00:17:34.000
The collars were tested and perfected on lurchers in Britain to make sure that they were small and light enough not to disturb their wearer.

00:17:36.000 --> 00:17:42.000
Then the collars were put on captive cheetahs to see if they could cope with the twisting one of a hunt.

00:17:42.000 --> 00:17:50.000
The results were excellent and the collars were ready for the ultimate test in the wild.

00:17:53.000 --> 00:18:02.000
Here was a chance to see if a wild cheetah's special adaptations to hunting really enabled it to run at 70 miles an hour.

00:18:02.000 --> 00:18:11.000
Wild cheetahs are faster than other larger cats like lions because of their lighter bones, an advantage in a short high-speed chase.

00:18:13.000 --> 00:18:22.000
They have big nostrils so they can take in large amounts of oxygen and an enlarged heart and lungs that increase circulation.

00:18:22.000 --> 00:18:30.000
Their long tails act like rudders to help them steer and assist their balance as they twist and turn.

00:18:31.000 --> 00:18:39.000
They need to be fast and maneuverable because the prey they hunt is extremely agile and able to change direction very quickly.

00:18:40.000 --> 00:18:44.000
A cheetah can mirror such changes of movements in an instant.

00:18:44.000 --> 00:18:51.000
But what would the GPS collars tell us about their speed?

00:18:52.000 --> 00:19:00.000
Data from the collars has revealed fascinating details about cheetahs' lives, how they hunt and exactly how fast they can run.

00:19:01.000 --> 00:19:07.000
The GPS measurements collected are accurate to what the collars are able to detect.

00:19:07.000 --> 00:19:18.000
How they hunt and exactly how fast they can run. The GPS measurements collected are accurate to within half a meter and can be precisely matched to satellite images of the area.

00:19:19.000 --> 00:19:23.000
So it's possible to see exactly what kind of terrain the cheetahs were hunting on.

00:19:24.000 --> 00:19:30.000
Here we can see an 11-hour day in the life of a cheetah and there it starts to hunt.

00:19:30.000 --> 00:19:35.000
The cheetah ran in one circular direction like this.

00:19:36.000 --> 00:19:43.000
The blue represents deceleration, getting slower here, and there the red when it gets faster and accelerates.

00:19:44.000 --> 00:19:53.000
And the arrows represent the power of the force on the cheetah's body as it swerves and there finally it made the kill.

00:19:53.000 --> 00:20:02.000
367 hunts were studied and the top speed of a chase was calculated to be 58 miles an hour.

00:20:08.000 --> 00:20:13.000
For more than half a century we have overestimated the cheetah's speed.

00:20:13.000 --> 00:20:24.000
It is nonetheless still the fastest animal on land and its greatest feat is its acceleration, four times that of Usain Bolt.

00:20:43.000 --> 00:21:06.000
The cheetah's legendary 70 miles an hour speed record is just a myth.

00:21:06.000 --> 00:21:12.000
But their true top speed of 58 miles an hour is still extraordinary.

00:21:13.000 --> 00:21:17.000
A body that is fine-tuned for hunting helps them run in a really remarkable way.

00:21:18.000 --> 00:21:30.000
But the cheetah's real impossible feat, so called, is the ability to change speeds so extremely quickly.

00:21:30.000 --> 00:21:37.000
And that makes it one of the most maneuverable animals alive, aren't you?

00:21:40.000 --> 00:21:50.000
We may have overestimated the abilities of the flea and the cheetah, but both exhibit remarkable feats of acceleration in their quest for food.

00:21:50.000 --> 00:21:59.000
The flea to hop onto a passing host and the cheetah to outmaneuver its prey.

00:22:20.000 --> 00:22:25.000
Thanks for watching!
